# Regression fixture for #98/#100/#85 -- three real bugs found chasing a
# broken --x64 pipeline this session, none of them catchable by the Rust
# cargo test suite (it has zero tests that invoke --x64 at all) or by
# `--ir-run`-only verification (#85's own first "fix" and #83's floor()
# fix were both only ever confirmed under --ir-run, which never
# exercises the real x64 codegen/assembly path). Wired into the standing
# language spec suite via a genuine interp-vs-x64/interp-vs-native
# comparison, the same pattern x64_bitwise_selftest_universal_print.
# feature already uses.
#
# #98 itself is checked separately below via the NATIVE `--patc` backend
# rather than --x64: #98 was a bug in the runtime HELPER functions
# (as_bits/as_int in codegen.rs/ops.rs, called by patc1.exe's OWN
# internal execution while it compiles something) rejecting a Float
# value outright and crashing the whole compile -- codegen.rs's as_bits
# is exactly what --patc's compiled output embeds, so this exercises
# the fixed code directly. A genuine, SEPARATE, still-open bug (#102)
# means the equivalent case under --x64 gives a wrong NUMBER (not a
# crash) for a different reason -- the x64 codegen's own `band` emits a
# raw bitwise AND on a Float's IEEE-754 bits instead of converting to
# int first. Not conflated here; #102 covers that one.

# #100: rt_dynamic_plus_obj had no "bool" branch at all, so a genuine
# Bool operand paired with a string silently dropped the string and
# fell through to `return 0`.
print("bool concat: " + true)
print(false + " tail")

# #85: print()'s own return value was a Bool `true` placeholder under
# --x64, not genuine Unit -- so type_of() misclassified it, and any `+`
# expecting Unit's "stringify to empty" behavior silently dropped the
# other operand instead.
let u = print("noop")
print(type_of(u))
print("prefix:" + u)
